@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+import styles from "./page.module.css";
+export const metadata = { title: "ANNA Neural Intelligence | Measured CPU CNN", description: "Hardware-aware neural training, held-out evaluation, INT8 tradeoffs and reproducible CPU benchmark evidence." };
+export default function NeuralPage() { return <main className={styles.main}>
+<a href="/anna">← ANNA overview</a><p className={styles.eyebrow}>ANNA NEXUS LABS LLC / OPE3.27N NEURAL FORGE</p>
+<h1>ANNA Neural Intelligence</h1><p className={styles.lead}>ANNA Neural Intelligence is a hardware-aware neural-network engineering project developed by Anna Nexus Labs LLC. It explores neural-network training, inference performance, optimization, quantization, and the relationship between machine-learning architectures and computing hardware.</p>
+<p className={styles.status}><strong>Locally trained and CPU measured.</strong> Evidence recorded October 13, 2026. Repository publication and this website update await owner review. The source was developed with AI assistance and tested locally.</p>
+<section><h2>A trained network alongside ANNA</h2><p>ForgeCNN is an original, standalone PyTorch convolutional model trained from random weights on UCI Optical Digits. ANNA 1.3 remains the separate multi-agent orchestration system. Its live provider experiment is unapproved, with $0 authorized spending. Connecting this classifier to ANNA is future work.</p></section>
+<section><h2>Model architecture</h2><p>38,282 trainable parameters. Two convolutions with ReLU, one pooling layer and a 64-unit classification head produce ten logits. Estimated convolution and linear work: 116,352 multiply-accumulates per image, excluding other operations.</p><figure><img src="/anna-neural/figures/architecture.svg" alt="ForgeCNN layers and tensor shapes from normalized 8 by 8 grayscale input through two convolutions, pooling, flatten and ten output logits" width="680" height="730" style={{maxWidth:680,margin:"0 auto"}} /><figcaption>The dense hidden layer owns most parameters. The second convolution owns most arithmetic work.</figcaption></figure></section>
+<section><h2>Training and held-out evaluation</h2><p>3,249 training images, 574 validation images and an official test file with 1,797 images. Normalization fits only training data. AdamW trains for 40 fixed epochs with batch size 64 and learning rate 0.002. The reference checkpoint minimizes validation loss at epoch 14. Test data does not select weights or training settings.</p><p>Three fixed seeds achieved 97.61%, 97.33% and 97.38% test accuracy. The predefined reference seed is 369. Repeating it reproduced identical selected weights and test metrics in this CPU environment.</p><figure><img src="/anna-neural/figures/learning-curves.svg" alt="Measured training and validation loss and accuracy over 40 epochs" width="1430" height="546" /><figcaption>Checkpoint selection uses validation loss, so the selected epoch can differ from the final epoch.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="/anna-neural/figures/confusion-matrix.svg" alt="FP32 reference confusion matrix for the held-out UCI test set, with digit classes zero through nine" width="780" height="676" /></figure></section>
+<section><h2>Precision and storage tradeoffs</h2><div className={styles.tableWrap}><table><caption>Official held-out test accuracy and serialized model state</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Variant</th><th scope="col">Accuracy</th><th scope="col">State size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">FP32</th><td>97.61%</td><td>156,412 bytes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">FP32 channels-last</th><td>97.61%</td><td>Same logical weights</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">INT8 x86</th><td>96.72%</td><td>47,854 bytes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>INT8 calibration uses 512 training images. The quantized state is 3.27 times smaller, but accuracy drops by 0.89 percentage points. Serialized state excludes the optimizer and runtime. INT8 checkpoint reload reproduced identical logits locally.</p></section>
+<section><h2>Measured CPU inference</h2><p>Xeon Platinum 8573C CPU in a shared Linux container, eight-core quota and an 8 GiB memory limit. PyTorch 2.5.1 CPU with Python 3.12. No GPU or custom Tesla hardware was available.</p><p>36 measured conditions compare FP32, channels-last FP32 and INT8 at batches 1, 8, 32 and 128 with 1, 2 and 4 intra-op threads. Each condition uses 30 warmups and five repetitions of 100 timed forwards. Preallocated synthetic input is used for timing; real held-out data is used for accuracy.</p><div className={styles.tableWrap}><table><caption>One-thread subset. Full matrix and variability available below.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Variant</th><th scope="col">Batch</th><th scope="col">Median ms</th><th scope="col">p95 ms</th><th scope="col">Images/s</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">fp32</th><td>1</td><td>0.0660</td><td>0.3932</td><td>7,764</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">int8</th><td>1</td><td>0.2695</td><td>0.9936</td><td>2,606</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32_channels_last</th><td>1</td><td>0.0553</td><td>0.2499</td><td>11,301</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32</th><td>8</td><td>0.2724</td><td>0.9089</td><td>21,430</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">int8</th><td>8</td><td>0.2818</td><td>0.8078</td><td>20,902</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32_channels_last</th><td>8</td><td>0.1769</td><td>0.5172</td><td>33,142</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32</th><td>32</td><td>1.4586</td><td>3.2477</td><td>17,786</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">int8</th><td>32</td><td>0.3714</td><td>0.8605</td><td>72,989</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32_channels_last</th><td>32</td><td>0.3507</td><td>1.0294</td><td>69,056</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32</th><td>128</td><td>2.0358</td><td>2.7771</td><td>60,340</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">int8</th><td>128</td><td>0.9667</td><td>1.6921</td><td>121,799</td></tr>
+<tr><th scope="row">fp32_channels_last</th><td>128</td><td>1.0718</td><td>1.9190</td><td>107,984</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure><img src="/anna-neural/figures/batch-performance.svg" alt="Measured one-thread forward latency and mean-based throughput versus batch size for FP32, channels-last FP32 and INT8" width="1430" height="585" /></figure><p>INT8 is slower at batch one and faster than contiguous FP32 at larger one-thread batches in these observations. Channels-last also improves several conditions. Shared-host timing variability limits performance conclusions. These ratios do not transfer automatically to another platform.</p></section>
+<section><h2>Verification and limitations</h2><ul><li>Locally verified: 29 neural tests, full training, checkpoint restoration, held-out metrics, quantization, CPU benchmarks and profiles.</li><li>Simulated: only ANNA's separate retained orchestration simulations; neural training results here are real local measurements.</li><li>Planned: larger datasets, additional hardware and separately reviewed ANNA integration.</li><li>Unmeasured: GPU and Tesla accelerator performance, energy, sustainable memory bandwidth and production behavior.</li><li>Public release: pending owner authorization. No employer endorsement or certification is claimed.</li></ul><p>Process peak RSS includes Python and PyTorch; it is not isolated activation memory. First-forward timing differs from full process startup. Strict FP32 training stays enabled, while INT8 inference has a scoped deterministic-enforcement exception for a pinned PyTorch kernel limitation.</p></section>
+<section><h2>Reproduce and inspect</h2><p><a href="/anna-neural/docs/ENGINEERING_REPORT.md" download>Engineering report</a> · <a href="/anna-neural/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md" download>Layer specifications</a> · <a href="/anna-neural/docs/BENCHMARK_METHOD.md" download>Benchmark methodology</a> · <a href="/anna-neural/docs/DATA_PROVENANCE.md" download>Dataset and license</a> · <a href="/anna-neural/benchmark-table.csv" download>Complete benchmark CSV</a> · <a href="/anna-neural/benchmark-summary.json" download>Measurement summary JSON</a></p><p>GitHub repository link: pending account selection and owner publication approval. Source, raw samples, model checkpoints and demonstration materials are in the review package. No placeholder repository URL is presented.</p><pre><code>python -m unittest discover -s tests -v{"\n"}python -m anna_neural.cli train --out checkpoints/my-run</code></pre><p>The UCI dataset has a CC BY 4.0 license. <a href="https://doi.org/10.24432/C50P49">Alpaydin and Kaynak, Optical Recognition of Handwritten Digits (1998)</a>. Small digit classification does not establish production vision performance.</p></section></main>; }
